## 1. Result

For each selected Hartshorne-Serre orbit member, the reverse space

\[
R_j=\operatorname{Ext}^1_X(L_j,E_j)
\simeq3(\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2),
\qquad \dim R_j=9,
\]

is not an undifferentiated search space. The explicit monad and the
normalization sequence of the \(K_{2,3}\) curve define a canonical quotient
test

\[
q_j:R_j\longrightarrow H^1(\Gamma_j,H D_j^{-1}),
\qquad D_j=\omega_{\Gamma_j}\otimes H^{-1}|_{\Gamma_j}.
\]

The exact result is

\[
\operatorname{rank}q_j=4,
\qquad
K_j:=\ker q_j\simeq\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2,
\qquad \dim K_j=5. \tag{1}
\]

Naturality of the local-to-global Ext spectral sequence makes \(q_j\) a
quotient shadow of the independent Yoneda obstruction

\[
\mu_E:R_j\longrightarrow\operatorname{Ext}^2_X(E_j,E_j).
\]

Consequently

\[
\operatorname{rank}\mu_E\geq4,
\qquad
\ker\mu_E\subseteq K_j,
\qquad
\dim\ker\mu_E\leq5. \tag{2}
\]

The remaining calculation is therefore a five-column normal/conormal
Yoneda block, not a nine-column unrestricted search. No nonzero
Maurer-Cartan class or physical endpoint is promoted by this theorem.

For the selected physical linearization, the downstream UST.G3M theorem
sharpens this classification result further: only the one-dimensional
conjugate-clock-fixed part of this five-space is an admissible immediate
candidate. The full block remains the non-equivariant deformation problem.

## 2. The Exact Monad

Let \(I_j=I_{\Gamma_j}\), \(L_j=\mathcal O_X(-H)\), and use the
locked q79 line typing

\[
A=(r=0),\qquad M=\mathcal O_X(A),\qquad
aT_j\in H^0(X,N),\qquad W=MN H^{-1}.
\]

Then

\[
D_j=\omega_{\Gamma_j}\otimes H^{-1}|_{\Gamma_j}=W|_{\Gamma_j}.
\]

The dual Hartshorne-Serre sequence and its locally free resolution are

\[
0\longrightarrow E_j\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X^{\oplus2}
\longrightarrow i_*D_j\longrightarrow0, \tag{3}
\]

\[
0\longrightarrow L_j\longrightarrow\kappa_j
\longrightarrow E_j\longrightarrow0. \tag{4}
\]

In the selected determinant atlas these combine into the monad

\[
0\longrightarrow H^{-1}
\xrightarrow{(r,aT_j,0,0)^t}
(M H^{-1})\oplus(N H^{-1})\oplus\mathcal O_X^{\oplus2}
\xrightarrow{(aT_j,-r,T_{j+1},T_{j+2})}
W\longrightarrow0. \tag{5}
\]

up to a simultaneous harmless sign convention. Every arrow now has its
declared line weight: multiplication by \(aT_j\) sends \(MH^{-1}\) to
\(W\), multiplication by \(r\) sends \(NH^{-1}\) to \(W\), and the two
theta generators are sections of \(W\). The composite is zero by the
displayed Koszul pair. Its middle cohomology is \(E_j\), and
the kernel of the second map is \(\kappa_j\). Thus the forward extension
class used in UST.G3I-G3K is the truncation class of this concrete monad.

Because \(T_{j+1},T_{j+2}\) generate \(D_j\) on the whole nodal curve,
(3) has the exact elementary-transform quotient

\[
0\longrightarrow I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2}
\longrightarrow E_j(H)
\longrightarrow i_*(H D_j^{-1})
\longrightarrow0. \tag{6}
\]

The subscripts record the two nontrivial clock linearizations carried by
the generator copies.

## 3. The Same-Base-Point Correction

The normalization of \(\Gamma_j\) consists of two vertical and three
horizontal elliptic components with dual graph \(K_{2,3}\). The line \(H\)
has multidegree

\[
(0,0;2,2,2),
\]

and \(D_j\) has multidegree

\[
(3,3;0,0,0).
\]

The two vertical components occur over

\[
q_+=(u=0,W=+3),\qquad q_-=(u=0,W=-3)
\]

on the degree-two normalization \(E_B\to\mathbf P^1\). They are distinct
points of \(E_B\), but they have the same image under that projection.
Since \(H\) is pulled back from the projection, the two evaluation
functionals on \(H^0(X,H)\) are proportional. After a common fiber
trivialization they are equal. Thus

\[
\operatorname{rank}\bigl(H^0(X,H)\to H|_{q_+}\oplus H|_{q_-}\bigr)=1,
\tag{7}
\]

not two. Equivalently, the selected section \(a\) spans the kernel and has
divisor \(q_++q_-\). This same-base-point fact is forced by the locked q79
equation and is the reason the quotient rank below is four rather than six.

The normalization evaluation matrix for \(H|_{\Gamma_j}\) has rank four.
It gives

\[
H^0(\Gamma_j,H)\simeq2\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2,
\qquad
H^1(\Gamma_j,H)\simeq2\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2. \tag{8}
\]

Combining (8) with the ideal sequence and the locked Leray groups on \(X\)
gives the exact clock representations below. Write
\(\operatorname{Reg}=\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2\).

| sheaf | \(H^0\) | \(H^1\) | \(H^2\) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| \(I_j(H)\) | \(\mathbf1\) | \(2\mathbf1\oplus\chi\oplus\chi^2\) | \(\operatorname{Reg}\) |
| \(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2}\) | \(\chi\oplus\chi^2\) | \(2\mathbf1\oplus3\chi\oplus3\chi^2\) | \(2\operatorname{Reg}\) |
| \(E_j(H)\) | \(\chi\oplus\chi^2\) | \(3\operatorname{Reg}\) | \(\mathbf1\) |
| \(H D_j^{-1}\) on \(\Gamma_j\) | \(\operatorname{Reg}\) | \(3\operatorname{Reg}\) | \(0\) |

For the last row, each positive-degree horizontal block has a one-dimensional
two-node evaluation image and a one-dimensional kernel. The two
negative-degree vertical components contribute six first-cohomology
directions. Hence

\[
h^0(HD_j^{-1})=3,
\qquad
h^1(HD_j^{-1})=3+6=9. \tag{9}
\]

## 4. Exact Long-Sequence Rank

The first map on \(H^0\) in (6) is the isomorphism
\(\chi\oplus\chi^2\to\chi\oplus\chi^2\) already computed from the two
\(r\)-kernel sections in UST.G3J. The next boundary injects
\(H^0(HD_j^{-1})\simeq\operatorname{Reg}\) into

\[
H^1(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2})
\simeq2\mathbf1\oplus3\chi\oplus3\chi^2.
\]

Its quotient, and therefore the image in \(R_j=H^1(E_j(H))\), is

\[
(2\mathbf1\oplus3\chi\oplus3\chi^2)-\operatorname{Reg}
=\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2. \tag{10}
\]

Exactness identifies this image with \(\ker q_j\). It has dimension five,
so

\[
\operatorname{rank}q_j=9-5=4. \tag{11}
\]

The rest of the long exact sequence independently checks the result. The
cokernel of \(q_j\) has representation
\(\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2\) and dimension five. It maps
isomorphically onto the kernel of the rank-one surjection

\[
H^2(I_j(H)^{\oplus2}_{\chi,\chi^2})
=2\operatorname{Reg}\longrightarrow H^2(E_j(H))=\mathbf1.
\]

## 5. Why This Is a Yoneda Obstruction

Since (4) is a locally free resolution of \(E_j\), sheaf Hom gives

\[
\mathcal Ext^1(E_j,E_j)
=\operatorname{coker}\left(
\kappa_j^\vee\otimes E_j\longrightarrow L_j^\vee\otimes E_j
\right)
=E_j(H)|_{\Gamma_j}. \tag{12}
\]

Tensoring (3) with \(\mathcal O_{\Gamma_j}\), and using the codimension-two
lci Tor sequence, gives

\[
0\longrightarrow D_j\otimes N_{\Gamma_j/X}^*
\longrightarrow E_j|_{\Gamma_j}
\longrightarrow D_j^{-1}\longrightarrow0.
\]

Because \(K_X\simeq\mathcal O_X\), adjunction gives
\(\det N_{\Gamma_j/X}=H D_j\). After twisting by \(H\), (12) has the
filtration

\[
0\longrightarrow N_{\Gamma_j/X}
\longrightarrow\mathcal Ext^1(E_j,E_j)
\longrightarrow H D_j^{-1}\longrightarrow0. \tag{13}
\]

The local-to-global Ext spectral sequence contains the natural edge map

\[
\operatorname{Ext}^2(E_j,E_j)
\longrightarrow H^1(\mathcal Ext^1(E_j,E_j)).
\]

Yoneda naturality identifies the composite

\[
R_j\xrightarrow{\mu_E}\operatorname{Ext}^2(E_j,E_j)
\longrightarrow H^1(\mathcal Ext^1(E_j,E_j))
\longrightarrow H^1(HD_j^{-1})
\]

with \(q_j\) in (6). A class detected by \(q_j\) cannot lie in
\(\ker\mu_E\). Equations (1) and (2) follow.

There is also an exact local-symbol statement. Since
\(I_j/I_j^2=N_{\Gamma_j/X}^*\), restriction of the two ideal copies in
(6) to \(\Gamma_j\) gives

\[
N_{\Gamma_j/X}^*\otimes H\otimes\mathcal O_{\Gamma_j}^{\oplus2}
\xrightarrow{(T_{j+1},T_{j+2})}
N_{\Gamma_j/X}^*\otimes H D_j
\simeq N_{\Gamma_j/X}. \tag{14}
\]

The last isomorphism is the rank-two identity
\(N^*\otimes\det N\simeq N\), using
\(H D_j=\det N_{\Gamma_j/X}\). Because the two theta sections generate
\(D_j\) everywhere, (14) is pointwise surjective, including at all six
nodes. Thus the open normal block is not caused by a local rank drop. It is
exactly the induced map on global Cech cohomology after quotienting the
three boundary directions in (10).

## 6. The Decisive Remaining Block

Define

\[
K_j=\ker q_j\simeq\mathbf1\oplus2\chi\oplus2\chi^2
\]

and restrict the independent obstruction:

\[
\nu_j:=\mu_E|_{K_j}. \tag{15}
\]

Its unresolved edge component lies in the normal term of (13). If
\(r_j=\operatorname{rank}\nu_j\), then

\[
0\leq r_j\leq5,
\qquad
\dim\ker\mu_E=5-r_j. \tag{16}
\]

Thus a rank-five result retires this locked Hartshorne-Serre bidirectional
cohesive route. Any smaller rank leaves an explicitly computable nonzero
kernel that must then be transported around the three-member shift and
integrated into a global Maurer-Cartan object. The calculation must use a
canonical Cech basis, selected node gluing, and the explicit monad maps. A
fitted \(5\times5\) matrix is not an accepted substitute.

